  "is_sufficient": false,
  "knowledge_gaps": [
    "The supplied baseline already uses a terminal/interior sampler. Its existing branch probability and reconstruction policy are unspecified, so the proposed 0.5 mixture may duplicate existing training rather than introduce a distinct intervention.",
    "Recording supervised-target counts does not control them. Sequence normalization equalizes example weights, while the branches still differ in target count, visible context and corruption density; any benefit cannot be attributed solely to serving alignment.",
    "The gradient audit lacks fixed sample counts, checkpoint timing, paired-prefix construction, parameter-block definitions and numerical thresholds for zero gradients. Evaluation-mode gradients describe a different dropout setting from training.",
    "The comparison lacks a concrete allocation within the remaining GPU budget, checkpoint-selection rules and a prespecified validation comparison. A terminal-only control would help interpretation if affordable, but additional arms must remain separate single-model trials.",
    "Originality 4/10 is defensible, but potential 7/10 is speculative without validation evidence. Difficulty 3/10 understates the complete integration, parity verification and reproducible gradient audit. The proposed objective also does not directly establish improved compatibility or current-purpose relevance."
  ],
  "follow_up_queries": [
    {
      "reason": "Clarify whether the experiment changes the baseline and identify which masking and sampling details must be documented before implementation.",
      "query": "How do the original BERT4Rec and Petrov–Macdonald Recency Sampling implementations combine last-item masking with random interior masking? Compare terminal-example probability, training-prefix sampling, mask density, visible context and loss reduction, and explain when a 50/50 terminal/reconstruction mixture is a distinct objective."
    },
    {
      "reason": "Separate nominal objective weighting from target exposure and make the proposed gradient audit reproducible without introducing adaptive optimization.",
      "query": "For a single BERT4Rec network trained on terminal-only and multi-target masked-reconstruction examples, how do per-target and per-sequence loss reductions affect expected gradients? What controls and reporting are needed for paired blockwise gradient cosine and norm measurements, including tied embeddings, dropout, zero gradients and unequal supervised-target counts?"
    },
    {
      "reason": "Strengthen attribution and validation reporting while respecting the fixed evaluator, seed and limited training budget.",
      "query": "What experimental designs do BERT4Rec reproducibility studies recommend for comparing masked reconstruction, terminal-only training and a fixed objective mixture under equal optimizer updates and limited GPU time? Address target-exposure versus compute matching, checkpoint selection, and paired sampled-HR@10 uncertainty when per-user validation outcomes are available."
    }
  ]
}
